    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d91\u0db8\u0dca.\u0d92. \u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0db1\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b8e\u0bae\u0bcd.\u0b8f. \u0b9a\u0bc1\u0bae\u0ba8\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba9\u0bcd) (The Hon. M.A. Sumanthiran) Hon. Deputy Chairman of Committees, as I began my speech on this issue, I was pointing out the fact that this is the first time, on many fronts, that a No-Confidence Motion of this nature is being debated in this House.\n\nHaving disclosed my interest in the matter, may I continue to say that this is the first time that a Determination of the Supreme Court in regard to a Bill had been blatantly violated by Parliament, unfortunately, and that is primarily the reason why this No-Confidence Motion against the Speaker has been presented. In every country, when the Legislature passes a law, there is a requirement that someone either endorses it or grants his or her assent to it and it is only upon that act that the said Bill would become law. Why is that requirement there in all the legislatures of the world? Then, we needed the Queen\u2019s assent, prior to Independence, the requirement was the Governor-General\u2019s assent, even now, in the Provincial Councils, the Governor must assent, here in Parliament, the Speaker and if there is a Referendum, the President must endorse. Why is that requirement there? The answer to that is there in the Constitution itself. Sir, let me read from Article 75 of the Constitution, certain provisions which talk about the legislative power of Parliament. The side note itself of Article 75 states \u201cLegislative power\u201d. Article 75 states, I quote:\n\n\u201cParliament shall have power to make laws, including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Constitution: Provided....\"\n\nSo, there is a proviso.\n\n\".... that Parliament shall not make any law -\n\n(a) suspending the operation of the Constitution or any part thereof; or\""
