    "chunk_text": "\u201cParliament shall have power to make laws, including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Constitution: Provided....\"\n\nSo, there is a proviso.\n\n\".... that Parliament shall not make any law -\n\n(a) suspending the operation of the Constitution or any part thereof; or\"\n\nThen, another prohibition is there in the Constitution, which states, I quote:\n\n\"(b) repealing the Constitution as a whole unless such law also enacts a new Constitution to replace it.\u201d\n\nYou cannot merely bring in a Bill to say the Constitution is repealed. Even if you pass it with a two-thirds majority, it would not become law; even if the Speaker endorses it, it does not become law.\n\nThen, Article 77. That is also important because the Speaker, in justifying his conduct in endorsing this Bill, says, \"I acted on the advice of the Attorney-General\". I must say that he can listen to the advice of the Attorney-General, but the law does not give the Attorney-General the power to endorse any Bill and the Speaker is also not expected to merely act exactly in the way that the Attorney-General advises him. If that is the case, the law would have said that the Attorney-General could endorse the Bill. The Speaker can listen to advice, but he is not a robot. He has to exercise his mind as to whether the proper legislative process has been followed. Why do I say that? It is because as per Article 77, the Attorney-General is supposed to examine every Bill, which states, I quote:\n\n\u201cIt shall be the due of the Attorney General to examine every Bill\u2026\u201d"
  },
