    "chunk_size_bytes": 1016,
    "chunk_text": "Then, Article 77. That is also important because the Speaker, in justifying his conduct in endorsing this Bill, says, \"I acted on the advice of the Attorney-General\". I must say that he can listen to the advice of the Attorney-General, but the law does not give the Attorney-General the power to endorse any Bill and the Speaker is also not expected to merely act exactly in the way that the Attorney-General advises him. If that is the case, the law would have said that the Attorney-General could endorse the Bill. The Speaker can listen to advice, but he is not a robot. He has to exercise his mind as to whether the proper legislative process has been followed. Why do I say that? It is because as per Article 77, the Attorney-General is supposed to examine every Bill, which states, I quote:\n\n\u201cIt shall be the due of the Attorney General to examine every Bill\u2026\u201d\n\n1501 1502\n\n2024 \u0db8\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd4 3\n\never received, challenging a Bill and that shows the importance of this particular bill - he came to Court."
