    "chunk_index": 91,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f9404149005ffae2efeb17ee0464701e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2049,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIt shall be the due of the Attorney General to examine every Bill\u2026\u201d\n\n1501 1502\n\n2024 \u0db8\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd4 3\n\never received, challenging a Bill and that shows the importance of this particular bill - he came to Court.\n\nThen, this is what Article 79, the next Article, states about the Speaker endorsing a Bill, I quote:\n\n\u201cThe Speaker shall endorse on every Bill passed by Parliament a certificate in the following form:-\n\nThis Bill (here state the short title of the Bill) has been duly passed by Parliament.\u201d\n\nHe must say that it has been, not passed, but has been \"duly\" passed by Parliament. That word \u201cduly\u201d is there not for nothing. Only when it is duly passed can the Speaker endorse it saying, \"This Bill....\", stating the short title of the Bill, \".....has been duly passed by Parliament.\" Sir, that Article further states, I quote:\n\n\u201cSuch certificate may also state the majority by which such Bill was passed:\u201d\n\nThen again, there is a proviso, which states, I quote:\n\n\"Provided that where by virtue of the provisions of Article 82 or Article 83 or Article 84 or Article 123(2) a special majority is required for the passing of a Bill, the Speaker shall certify such Bill only if\u2026.\u201d\n\nI stress the words \u201conly if \".\n\n\"..... such Bill has been passed with such special majority:\u201d\n\nSo, if the Supreme Court says that this Clause cannot be passed without a special majority, as Article 79 states, the Speaker can endorse it as having been duly passed only if - unfortunately, our Speaker does not seem to know what is meant by \u201conly if\u201d - it is passed by a special majority. Then, it goes on to state, I quote:\n\n\u201cProvided further that where by virtue of Article 83, the Bill or any provision thereof requires the approval of the People at a Referendum, such certificate shall further state that the Bill or such provision shall not become law until approved by the People at a Referendum.\u201d\n\nBut, that is not relevant here. It is after Articles 75, 77 and 79 that Article 80 appears and Article 80(3) states, I quote:"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-03-19-2024-03-19",
    "num": "2024-03-19",
    "date_str": "2024-03-19",
    "description": "Hansard of March 19, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1712654406078311.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-03-19-2024-03-19-0092",
    "chunk_index": 92,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "cf1d791f4f23970c8d24b92373a73aa3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2018,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cProvided further that where by virtue of Article 83, the Bill or any provision thereof requires the approval of the People at a Referendum, such certificate shall further state that the Bill or such provision shall not become law until approved by the People at a Referendum.\u201d\n\nBut, that is not relevant here. It is after Articles 75, 77 and 79 that Article 80 appears and Article 80(3) states, I quote:\n\nIt states only \"Where a Bill becomes law....\".\n\nIt does not state that upon the Certificate of the Speaker, a Bill becomes law, no. Then, it states, I quote:\n\n\u201cWhere a Bill becomes law ....., no court or tribunal shall inquire into, pronounce upon or in any manner call in question, the validity of such Act on any ground whatsoever.\u201d\n\nThere was reference by the Proposer of the Motion to the law in Singapore with regard to online safety. Let us say the Speaker went on a trip to Singapore. He found the relevant Act of Singapore. He thinks, \u201cOh, this is a very good law. We must also have it in our country.\u201d He buys a few copies and comes here. He sits in his Chamber and endorses it and signs it. Then, does it become law? No, it does not. It becomes law only if it is duly passed by Parliament.\n\nIf the Supreme Court has determined that it shall have a two-thirds majority for it to be passed, let us see what happens. And where do we find that provision? That is in Article 123(4) of the Constitution. Article 123(4) gives the Supreme Court the power to determine any Bill that is challenged before it and it also states, I quote:\n\n\u201cWhere any Bill, or the provision of any Bill, has been determined or is deemed to have been determined, to be inconsistent with the Constitution, such Bill or such provision shall not be passed.....\"\n\n\"Shall not be passed\".\n\n\".......except in the manner stated in the determination of the Supreme Court:\n\nProvided that it shall be lawful for such Bill to be passed after such amendment as would make the Bill cease to be inconsistent with the Constitution.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
