    "chunk_index": 93,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "30ecab4dfe6175eaeda578b93f8ba58b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "\".......except in the manner stated in the determination of the Supreme Court:\n\nProvided that it shall be lawful for such Bill to be passed after such amendment as would make the Bill cease to be inconsistent with the Constitution.\u201d\n\n1503 1504\n\n\u0db4\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd41\n\nmajority of the whole number of Members of Parliament including those not present, then, they could have gone ahead because the Supreme Court has determined that it requires a special majority. But, if it was going to be passed by a simple majority, as in this case - it was passed only by a simple majority - then, those Amendments had to be brought in at the Committee Stage. But, several of those Amendments prescribed by the Supreme Court were not there.\n\nThe Government is required to place on the table of every Member of Parliament the Committee Stage Amendments that are proposed to be made before 12 noon. It was a two-day Debate on the Bill. Before 12 noon of the first day, a list of Amendments was tabled and because the list of Amendments was tabled, I was able to examine those Amendments along with the Supreme Court Determination and I found that 13 recommendations or Amendments prescribed by the Supreme Court were not there in the list of Amendments that the Government was moving. When the Committee Stage commenced, the Hon. Dayasiri Jayasekara and I raised objection and said that those Amendments were not there in the list of Amendments that was tabled before us. There was a huge protest. Sir, it is not that the Speaker\u2019s attention was not drawn to this; it was drawn and there was a big protest. Most of us came down to the Well of the House and told him, \u201cYou cannot do this. You are violating the Supreme Court Determination.\u201d I even gave a document in writing, listing those 13 points."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-03-19-2024-03-19",
