    "chunk_index": 94,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "00fe6a8dda33756480520b29ce2ba3ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1546,
    "chunk_text": "The Government is required to place on the table of every Member of Parliament the Committee Stage Amendments that are proposed to be made before 12 noon. It was a two-day Debate on the Bill. Before 12 noon of the first day, a list of Amendments was tabled and because the list of Amendments was tabled, I was able to examine those Amendments along with the Supreme Court Determination and I found that 13 recommendations or Amendments prescribed by the Supreme Court were not there in the list of Amendments that the Government was moving. When the Committee Stage commenced, the Hon. Dayasiri Jayasekara and I raised objection and said that those Amendments were not there in the list of Amendments that was tabled before us. There was a huge protest. Sir, it is not that the Speaker\u2019s attention was not drawn to this; it was drawn and there was a big protest. Most of us came down to the Well of the House and told him, \u201cYou cannot do this. You are violating the Supreme Court Determination.\u201d I even gave a document in writing, listing those 13 points.\n\nSir, I will give you some clear examples. Clauses 20 (1), 29(6), 30(1), 31(1) of the Bill contained penal provisions regarding doubling of punishment in the event of a second or subsequent conviction. In page 42 of the Supreme Court Determination, this is what the Court held, I quote:\n\n\u201cA careful consideration of the penal sections in the Bill shows that the courts are vested with the discretion to impose punishments as the said Clauses set out minimum and maximum punishments."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-03-19-2024-03-19",
    "num": "2024-03-19",
    "date_str": "2024-03-19",
    "description": "Hansard of March 19, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1712654406078311.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-03-19-2024-03-19-0095",
    "chunk_index": 95,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "d8674cb4e9102d626bdb50efe5b89fcf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, I will give you some clear examples. Clauses 20 (1), 29(6), 30(1), 31(1) of the Bill contained penal provisions regarding doubling of punishment in the event of a second or subsequent conviction. In page 42 of the Supreme Court Determination, this is what the Court held, I quote:\n\n\u201cA careful consideration of the penal sections in the Bill shows that the courts are vested with the discretion to impose punishments as the said Clauses set out minimum and maximum punishments.\n\nIf you read the Act that we have now, all of those Clauses are there as they were in the Bill; there is no change in those provisions on doubling of the punishment in case of a second or subsequent offence. The Supreme Court had very clearly said that those are violative of the Constitution, that you cannot pass it except by a special majority, but it was passed with a simple majority and the Speaker had endorsed it. I myself brought all of these six matters to the Speaker\u2019s attention by an e-mail I sent and said, \u201cDo not endorse the Bill because there are still some issues on which there is the Supreme Court's clear Determination that a two-thirds majority is required. This was not passed by a two-thirds majority. So, do not endorse the Bill.\u201d That is why the requirement of an endorsement is there and not for the Speaker to listen to the Attorney-General and say, \u201cHe advised me. So, I am signing.\u201d Then, we do not need a Speaker; we can have the Attorney-General sit in that Chair. The law has vested in the Speaker the power to endorse a Bill and he endorses it saying, \u201cIt is duly passed by Parliament\u201d, not saying that it was just \"passed by Parliament\". So, all of these provisions I just read out clearly state that you cannot endorse a Bill unless it has complied with the Determination of the Supreme Court. I only gave one example. There are several such examples."
  },
