    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 (\u0dbb\u0daf\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dad\u0dba) \u0dc4\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dad\u0dc2 \u0daf \u0dc3\u0dd2\u0dbd\u0dca\u0dc0\u0dcf \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 (\u0b95\u0bb2\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf) \u0bb9\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0bb7 \u0ba4 \u0bc6\u0bbf\u0bb2\u0bcd\u0bb5\u0bc6\u0bbe) (The Hon. (Dr.) Harsha de Silva) In the case of the Supreme Court, Sir, sometimes, judges recuse themselves due to personal reasons; if it is a personal matter, they do not want to sit in judgment. I do not know under what circumstances those two members of the Constitutional Council abstained. It could even be on a personal matter. In a situation like that, a judge could recuse himself or herself and a another judge could be asked to sit in judgment. But, in this case, how do we deal with it? What if there would be personal issues?\n\nIn this case, qualifications have already been given in the Constitution. In addition to that, there are Rules for the Constitutional Council. I think the Hon. Member has not gone through those. The Constitutional Council drafted their Rules running into 56 pages! At a particular time, we were blamed, saying that the President was not allowing those to be printed. But, that, we did with a purpose. Can you understand, Rules running into 56 pages? It is an insult to Parliament! Thereafter, we redrafted the Rules. Now, those run into only four pages. The Rules has specified that in the event there is a conflict\n\nof interest for a the member of the Council, he must record it saying, \"I have a conflict of interest in this regard\", but still, he has to take a decision. So, while recording it, he has to take a decision because - in the court, if one judge is not available, we can get another judge - you cannot do so in the Constitutional Council as there is only one Constitutional Council; that is the apex body.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dba\u0ddd\u0da2\u0dba \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0d9a \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf\u0db4\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b95\u0bc1\u0bb4\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bcd \u0ba4\u0bb5\u0bbf\u0bc6\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb3\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Deputy Chairman of Committees) Thank you, Hon. Minister. Next, the Hon. (Prof.) G.L. Peiris. You have 12 minutes.\n\nOrder, please! Before he starts, will an Hon. Member propose the Hon. S.M.M. Muszhaaraff to the Chair?"
  },
