    "md5": "f90996e803357de95030c1640f037f85",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1357,
    "chunk_text": "\"5. The 5th Respondent is ordered to pay as compensation a sum of Rs. 500,000/- (Rupees Five-Hundred Thousand).\n\nThe 1st, 2nd, 4th and 5th Respondents are to pay the aforementioned sums, within six months from the date of judgment, out of their personal funds.\"\n\nThere is no rule to say, other than the accepted practice or what is expected of them, that they should vote either \u201cYes\u201d or \u201cNo\u201d. The accepted practice coming down from the Thirteenth Century and very specifically illustrated in Erskine May is also expected to be followed by the Hon. Speaker. So, in this case, for the Speaker to suggest that those members who abstained fall either under \u201cAyes\u201d or \u201cNoes\u201d cannot be done. Abstinence means abstinence; it does not mean \u201cYes\u201d or \u201cNo\u201d.\n\nI recall that at one point, when there was a vote on a Resolution in Geneva, in favour of the Resolution, there was a certain number and against it, there was another number. Then, there were certain other countries which abstained and the Government at that time added the number which abstained to the number of votes they received and said, \u201cWe won the Resolution\u201d. Actually, it was the other way round. The Resolution was brought in by someone else and the Government said, \"We defeated the Resolution\", which was not the case. The United Nations did not accept that position."
  },
